<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n27144" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">lūdĭa</span></b> , ae,
			f.
		1. ludius. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>An actress, a female stage-dancer</i>, <i><span class="en">Mart. 5,
				25, 10</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>A female gladiator, a gladiator's wife</i> or <i>mistress</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quid vidit, propter quod ludia dici Sustinuit,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Juv.
					6, 103</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">quae ludia sumpserit umquam hos habitus?</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id. 6,
					266</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
    <div class="alpheios-lex-alph-source">From <u>
            <u>A Latin Dictionary</u>
        </u> (Charlton T. Lewis, Charles Short, Oxford, Clarendon Press, 1879) </div>
</div>